## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about data deletion in log-based, multi-tenant systems; covers tombstone propagation, privacy guarantees, auditability, and verification under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Tombstone semantics, cross-topic propagation, and per-tenant isolation
- Idempotence, retention versus deletion semantics
- Audit trails and verification under failure conditions
- Handling cross-topic joins while preserving deletion markers
- Compression-aware deletion propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
function createTombstone(tenantId, key, deletionTimestamp) {
  return {
    tenantId,
    key,
    deletionTimestamp,
    isTombstone: true
  };
}

function propagateTombstone(tombstone, pipeline) {
  pipeline.forEach(stage => {
    stage.emit(tombstone);
    stage.compress(); // Preserve tombstone through compression
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical schema evolution handling in KCNA beginners: safe deserialization by versioning, backward compatibility, DLQ handling, and testability with canaries.

## Key Concepts
- Schema versioning
- In-memory deserializer registry
- Backward compatibility
- Dead-letter routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal example
type Message = { payload: any };
type Deser = (payload: any)=>any;

const registry = new Map<number, Deser>();
registry.set(1, (p)=> ({id: p.id, t: p.time}));
registry.set(2, (p)=> ({id: p.id, ts: p.time, meta: p.meta}));

function deserialize(record: { payload: any }) {
  const v = Number(record.payload?.schema_version ?? 1);
  const deser = registry.get(v) ?? registry.get(1);
  if (!deser) throw new Error('No deserializer');
  return deser(record.payload);
}
```

